 { In the first section we focused on helping us to judge the strength of an outpost. Oftentimes, however, there is only one good outpost, or we are clear on which outpost we should aim for. In this case, the important thing is to find the right maneuver to it. Can you spot the ideal outpost for one of White's minor pieces, and maneuver towards it? } 1. { -KEY- } Na3 $1 { Given the structure, White identifies a fourth rank outpost on the queenside. This is a very easy decision since White's knight was the only undeveloped minor piece and a3 is the only available square. } Qc7 2. Nc4 $16 { Correct! White has a much better position, with pressure against the d4 pawn. Over the coming moves, White will play Qd1-f3 and perhaps push e3-e4 and d2-d3. This would increase central control while fixing Black's bad structure. It's true that this approach would leave an outpost on c3, but it's not a concern since neither of Black's minor pieces can easily reach that square. In the next example, we'll show a typical maneuver with a central outpost as the target. } 
