 { Our introductory example was straight forward. Most maneuvering that you will have to find will be more complicated. However, in many cases you will be aided by the fact that there are certain maneuvers that appear very frequently. In the current position, can you find the three-move maneuver to the outpost on d6? } 1. { -KEY- } Nd2 $1 { The knight on f3 was restricted by Black's pawn chain. Now it begins its journey to the d6 square, where it will pose a major threat to the Black position. } ( 1. Rd6 $2 { The rook is the wrong piece for the outpost, as it can be immediately pushed back after } Bf8 $11 { White must retreat, wasting a couple of tempi. Black can use this time to regroup and hold the balance. } ) 
Rad8 $2 { Black plays a natural move, but now will have no good reply against the threat to invade on d6. } ( 1... a5 $1 { Black was in positional trouble either way, but the active pawn break offered some chances to generate counterplay. } ) 
2. Nc4 $1 { Black is presented with the difficult choice of picking which of their bishops to give up for the knight. } Bf8 ( { Preparing to give up the dark-squared bishop in exchange for the knight. After } 2... Bxc4 3. Bxc4 $16 { White's position is near-winning as they now have the bishop pair and dominate the light squares. } ) 
3. Nd6 $1 { Correct! The knight reaches the end of its journey, forcing Black to give up the bishop pair. } Bxd6 4. cxd6 $18 { This move is even stronger than Rxd6 since the double attack will cost Black a pawn. } 
