 { Sometimes, it will be necessary to identify even longer maneuvers. Here, Black has an advantage because the central structure leaves White with a very passive dark-squared bishop. However, to improve their position, they must find a long maneuver. Can you spot the route that Black found for their knight? } 1... { -KEY- } Nf8 $1 { The beginning of a long sequence. The closed structure gives Black enough time to play the slow but powerful knight maneuver to d5. } 2. h4 { White is one move too slow to control the g6 square. If it were their move, they could play h4-h5 here and keep the Black knight under control for now. } Ng6 $1 3. h5 { White grabs space on the kingside. } Ne7 4. Bc1 $1 { Improving the bishop. } Nd5 { Correct! Now the knight is ideally posted on the central square, and White will have to defend very precisely to keep any hope of surviving alive. } 5. e3 $6 ( 5. Bf4 $1 { kept better chances of holding, though even then after } g5 $1 6. hxg6 Rg7 { the g-file would be forced open and Black's attack would likely succeed. } ) 
a4 { First Black locks down the structure. } 6. b4 g6 $1 7. hxg6 Rg7 8. Rg1 Rxg6 9. Kf2 Qg7 $19 { Black is winning. They will likely triple up forces on the g-file, and, if needed, push the h-pawn up to open more lines of attack. } 
