 { The examples that you have been quizzed on so far are the kind of situations you will see time and time again. However, every once in a while an opportunity for a strong piece maneuver will be cleverly disguised! Take a look at this crazy possibility that I missed in a game of mine against GM Manolache. } 22. { -KEY- } Be4 $3 { An incredible idea, which did not even cross my mind. White activates his passive bishop at all costs! } ( 22. Na4 $1 { is also powerful, hunting the b6 square and looking to eliminate Black's bishop before it hits the long diagonal. } ) 
 ( 22. Qg2 $2 { This was what I played in the game Astaneh Lopez,A - Manolache,M (2005). However, after } Bd7 $1 { I realised that I cannot take the 'free' pawn since after } 23. Qxb7 $2 Qh6 $1 24. Rce1 Bc6 { Black will play ...Nf3+ next, winning an exchange. } ) 
fxe4 ( 22... Rb8 { If Black ignores the bishop, then White can slot it back to g2 when not only is the piece much improved, but White's king begins to feel a lot safer. } 23. Bg2 $16 ) 
23. Nxe4 { This is the logic behind the piece sacrifice. White gives back the extra piece he started with, in order to target the weak square on f6. } Kh8 { Because Black only has an extra pawn, they can't afford to lose an exchange after 24.Nf6+. } ( 23... Rf8 $2 { poses little resistance after } 24. Rxf8 Qxf8 25. Rf1 Qe7 26. Nf6 Kg7 27. Qb2 $18 { Black's dark square weaknesses are too severe, and attempts to defend the knight with either ...Qd6 or ...Qc7 lose immediately to Ne8+. } ) 
24. Nf6 Rf8 25. g5 $1 { The weak square on f6 becomes an outpost! } Nd7 $6 { Black has to challenge the outpost directly. } ( 25... h6 $6 { is futile, since at a minimum White can support the pawn with } 26. h4 $18 ) 
26. Neg4 $1 { Bringing in fresh supplies! } Nxf6 27. Rxf6 $1 { This is even stronger than capturing with the knight, as White looks to exchange rooks to strip Black's king of defending pieces. } Rxf6 28. gxf6 $1 ( 28. Nxf6 { is also strong, but even stronger is to transform the outpost into a dangerous passed pawn. Calculating specifics when possible is a good idea, as concrete lines will always trump general rules. } ) 
Qc7 29. Qe5 $1 { White spots that the endgame is losing for Black because of his lack of development. } Qxe5 30. Nxe5 { White's knight stops Black from being able to develop. } b6 31. Rd1 Bb7 { Black has to develop, but now White breaks in along the seventh rank. } 32. Rd7 Be4 33. Nf7+ Kg8 34. Nh6+ Kh8 ( 34... Kf8 { loses the h7 pawn, but now Black gets quickly mated. } ) 
35. Rg7 $1 Rf8 36. Rg8+ $1 Rxg8 37. Nf7# { Smothered mate! While tactical vision helped White to kill the game, it was an awareness of piece maneuvering and the power of advanced outposts that set up the conditions for an attack. The typical maneuvers that we saw in the first few examples are essential to quickly spot and capitalise on. Expert level players spot these common positional patterns with the same speed as tactical patterns like a double attack or a back-rank mate. Of course, you will not always have an outpost ready to be occupied. Sometimes you will have to search for opportunities to create one yourself! Let's turn to this topic next. } 
