 { At the amateur level, the most common way that outposts are created is through unforced errors. } 1. { -KEY- } c4 $2 { A huge positional mistake, gifting Black an outpost on d4. } N7e6 $1 { Black immediately targets the new weakness. } 2. b3 $2 { White wants to play Bb2 next, when the d4 square would be under enough piece control. However, they are one move too slow. } ( 2. Re1 { White needed to play this hard-to-find move to prepare for the invasion on d4, but even still their position is hard to repair. } Nd4 3. Qd1 Rd8 4. Nxd4 exd4 { ...Rxd4 would also have led to an edge, but here it makes sense for Black to transform the outpost into a well-defended passed pawn and kick back White's knight in the process. } 5. Nf1 Be6 6. b3 a4 $17 { It's worth nothing how Black drums up this quickfire initiative where White is just forced to react nonstop to all of Black's moves as their position degrades across the board. That's a common phenomenon in chess, when an initial defect of a position leads to a chain reaction and problems appear everywhere else. } ) 
Nd4 $1 3. Qb1 { White retreats the queen to b1 in order to keep defending the e4 pawn. } ( 3. Nxd4 $2 { loses material after } exd4 4. Nf1 d3 $19 ) 
Ne2+ $1 4. Kh2 Nc3 $19 { After White's positional blunder on move one, Black manages to win easily by moving the same piece four times in a row! In this game we saw that many times there are going to be unforced errors that you can exploit. However, sometimes if you want to build a strategy around an outpost you have to take initiative and force the creation of an outpost yourself by changing the structure. This is what the rest of this section will be about. } 
 *

