 { This is a position taken from the game Wipperman,T - Johansson,T (2005). Can you spot the move that White played in order to create an outpost? } 19. { -KEY- } Nxd5 $1 { Correct! The knight exchange strengthens Black centre, but the more important factor is the outpost that will appear on b5. } cxd5 20. Nc3 $1 { Targeting the d5 pawn while eyeing up the outpost on b5. } ( 20. Bf4 $6 { It would have been tempting to retreat the bishop on e5 in order to avoid losing what appears to be a stronger piece than Black's knight. However, after } Bxf4 21. Nxf4 e6 $11 { the position is equal since White's knight is too far removed from the b5 outpost to cause any damage. } ) 
Nxe5 $2 { Black grabs the bishop, but White must have spotted that this new change in structure would also play in his favour. } 21. dxe5 e6 { Black needed to defend the d5 pawn, but now White has gained a new outpost on d6. } 22. Nb5 $1 { IM Wipperman must have noticed the link between the b5 and d6 outposts when playing 19. Nxd5 in our starting position. } Qb6 23. f4 { Having established a bind on the queenside, White shifts his focus to the kingside where he will go for pawn breaks to open up lines of attack. } ( { White is in no rush to jump in with the knight, since after } 23. Nd6 Red8 $13 { the absence of White's knight from the b5 square means that he has to attend to the attack against the b2 pawn. } ) 
Rec8 $6 ( { An imprecise move, leaving Black's rook on a8 without use. } 23... Rac8 { was stronger, but the position would remain challenging. } ) 
24. b3 $1 { Taking away any counterplay from Black. } Rc6 25. g4 $1 { White makes contact with the h5 pawn, threatening to open up lines of attack against the king. } hxg4 ( 25... h4 { might have held out for longer, but it would be only a matter of time before White collects the stranded pawn. } ) 
26. hxg4 { Capturing on g4 has created an open h-file that White can now use in his attack. } Rac8 27. c3 $1 { Wipperman's play is extremely instructive. Instead of using his outposted knight as an offensive piece, he uses it to glue his queenside together. The stable configuration allows him to shut out all three of Black's heavy pieces. } Qd8 { Unable to make any progress, Black gives up on the queenside and reroutes the queen to the centre. } 28. g5 { Grabbing space. } Bg7 29. Rh1 Kf8 30. Rh7 $1 { White finally invades. Black is beaten on all three sides of the board, so there is nothing to do. } Ke7 { Black's last trick meets an immediate refutation. } 31. Rxg7 Qh8 { Black was banking on trapping White's rook. } 32. Qxg6 $18 { Since the f7 pawn is pinned, Black resigned. This game showed how you can change the structure to build outposts, and how outposts can be just as useful in defense as they are in attack. Here, the knight was not used in the attack, but rather to shut down Black's counterplay on the queenside. } 
