 { Sometimes the way to change the structure is through one, or more, pawn exchanges. } 1. { -KEY- } bxc6 $1 { A critical in-between move, using the fact that Black's knight is attacked. } ( { In the game, White failed to sense the danger and dropped the knight back: } 1. Nd2 $2 e4 $1 2. cxd5 cxd5 3. Na2 fxg3 4. fxg3 Be5 $19 { White cannot hold their position together. For example: } 5. Qe1 Qc2 $19 { The stranded knight is lost. } ) 
bxc6 { Black does not have time to capture on e4 yet, since their d7 knight was under fire. } 2. cxd5 $1 { A second pawn exchange completes White's idea. } cxd5 { Through the sequence of pawn exchanges, White has secured an outpost on b5. } 3. Nb5 $1 { Now, White uses the outpost in order to save the piece while gaining time against the Black queen. } Qd8 4. Ned6 $11 { With the d6 square under control White is able to move the knight forward, generating threats against the Black position. The position remains balanced, but now it is Black that has to be careful to weather the initiative. } 
 *

