 { There is one more common method that you can use to create an outpost: the use of pawn breaks. } 1... { -KEY- } b5 $1 { Black is looking to build an outpost on d4, using the break with ...b5-b4 to dislodge White's c3 pawn. } ( 1... d4 $6 { This move also forces an outpost, but is less strong. Play might have continued } 2. Bd2 dxc3 3. Qxc3 { when Black has gained the d4 outpost. The problem is that after } Nd4 $6 4. N3xd4 cxd4 5. Qb2 $15 { White is well-placed to cover the c3 outpost, while Black's minor pieces will not be able to access it any time soon. The strength of an outpost depends on how easily it can be occupied. } ) 
2. Ng3 { White's best try is to retreat in order to support the e4 pawn against the pressure. } b4 { Finally, Black makes contact with the c3 pawn, forcing an outpost. } 3. cxb4 ( 3. c4 $6 { would leave Black with a dominant position after } dxe4 4. dxe4 Nd4 $17 { when Black's outpost is particularly strong as it is supported by both the e5 and c5 pawns. In this course, such outposts are termed 'power outposts' and will be discussed in a separate section later. One of the advantages of such outposts is that once White captures on d4, Black will leave himself with a protected passed pawn after recapturing. } ) 
d4 $1 { Securing the c3 outpost. } ( 3... cxb4 $2 { would be a big mistake, since here White could crack open the centre and get back in the game with } 4. d4 $11 ) 
4. Bd2 cxb4 { Strengthening control over the c3 outpost. } ( 4... Nxb4 $5 { would also be a good move, though for different strategic reasons. In this case, after } 5. Qb1 Qa6 { Black would have tremendous queenside pressure, while } 6. Bxb4 { would once again land White in trouble since after } cxb4 $17 { Black has a space advantage and a powerful c3 outpost that he can hope to use in the coming moves. } ) 
5. Rc1 Rc8 6. Qb2 a5 $17 { Black will drive his knight to the c3 square in the coming moves, while White has no good way of contesting this plan. } 
